SCENARIO
You are building a food delivery app that tracks four values per order: the order total (Rs
499.50), the delivery distance (7.3 km), the payment method ('UPI'), and whether the
restaurant is currently accepting orders (True).

Question: Identify the correct Python data type for each of the four values above. Then explain
why assigning the wrong type — for example, storing Rs 499.50 as an integer — would cause an
incorrect result when applying a 10% discount calculation.


Explaination:

The order total, Rs 499.50, should have the float data type because it contains a decimal value. The delivery distance, 7.3 km, should also be a float because it is a decimal number. The payment method, 'UPI', should be a string because it is text, while True should be a boolean because it represents whether the restaurant is accepting orders or not.

If Rs 499.50 is stored as an integer instead of a float, the decimal part may be lost and the value may become Rs 499. When a 10% discount is calculated, the discount would then be calculated on Rs 499 instead of the actual order value of Rs 499.50. This would give an incorrect discount and final payable amount. Therefore, using the correct data type is important for getting accurate results in billing calculations.